." XLOG(3) Manual Page
.TH XLOG 3 "2026" "Extern Library" "Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
xlog_vcprint, xlog_cprint, xlog_print, xlog_vprint, XLOGV, XLOGD, XLOGI, XLOGW, XLOGE, XLOGF - cross-platform logging and diagnostic utilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "xlog.h"

XSTDAPI int XCALLAPI xlog_vcprint(int prio,int line,const char *file,const char *tag,const char *fmt,va_list ap);

XSTDAPI int XCALLAPI xlog_cprint(int prio,int line,const char *file,const char *tag,const char *fmt,...);

#define xlog_vprint(prio,tag,fmt,ap) \
xlog_vcprint(prio,**LINE**,**FILE**,tag,fmt,ap)

#define xlog_print(prio,tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_cprint(prio,**LINE**,**FILE**,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#define XLOGE(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_ERROR,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#define XLOGI(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_INFO,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#define XLOGV(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_VERBOSE,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#define XLOGW(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_WARN,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#define XLOGF(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_FATAL,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#ifdef DEBUG
# define XLOGD(tag,fmt,...) xlog_print(XLOG_DEBUG,tag,fmt,##__VA_ARGS__)
#else
# define XLOGD(tag,fmt,...) 0
#endif
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B xlog
library provides a lightweight, cross-platform logging interface for the
Extern Library.

It provides standardized log priorities, platform-independent logging
macros, automatic source file and line information, tagged messages,
variadic and
.B va_list
logging interfaces, timestamped output on desktop and POSIX platforms,
Android Logcat integration, Windows-compatible time handling, debug-only
logging, and configurable output through
.B XLOG_STREAM .

The API is designed to provide a single logging interface across Windows,
POSIX-compatible systems, and Android.

.SH 1. OVERVIEW
.B xlog.h
provides the logging and diagnostic interface used by the Extern Library.

The interface includes:

.IP (bu 2
Standardized log priorities.
.IP (bu 2
Platform-independent logging macros.
.IP (bu 2
Automatic source file and line information.
.IP (bu 2
Tagged log messages.
.IP (bu 2
Variadic and
.B va_list
logging interfaces.
.IP (bu 2
Timestamped output on desktop and POSIX platforms.
.IP (bu 2
Android Logcat integration.
.IP (bu 2
Windows-compatible time handling.
.IP (bu 2
Debug-only logging.
.IP (bu 2
Configurable output through
.BR XLOG_STREAM .

The API is intended to hide platform-specific logging details while
providing a consistent C interface.

.SH 2. HEADER DEPENDENCIES
The public header includes:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include "xstdio.h"
.fi

The implementation additionally relies on:

.nf
#include <stdarg.h>
#include <time.h>
.fi

The logging interface uses:

.IP (bu 2
.B FILE
and standard streams.
.IP (bu 2
.B va_list
for variadic logging.
.IP (bu 2
.B time_t
and
.B struct tm
for timestamps.
.IP (bu 2
.BR strftime (3)
for timestamp formatting.

.SH 3. DEFAULT CONFIGURATION
The logger provides two configurable defaults:

.nf
#define XLOG_DEFAULT_TAG "XLOG"
#define XLOG_STREAM      stderr
.fi

.SS Default tag
When a caller supplies a NULL tag, the logger uses:

.nf
XLOG
.fi

For example:

.nf
XLOGI(NULL, "Library initialized");
.fi

produces output similar to:

.nf
2026-09-30 18:30:42 [INFO]	XLOG (main.c:42) Library initialized
.fi

.SS Output stream
On non-Android platforms, output is written to:

.nf
stderr
.fi

through:

.nf
#define XLOG_STREAM stderr
.fi

Applications may redefine
.B XLOG_STREAM
before including
.B xlog.h
if a different
.B FILE *
is required.

.SH 4. LOG PRIORITIES
.B XLogPriority
defines the available logging levels:

.nf
typedef enum XLogPriority {
XLOG_UNKNOWN = 0,
XLOG_DEFAULT,
XLOG_VERBOSE,
XLOG_DEBUG,
XLOG_INFO,
XLOG_WARN,
XLOG_ERROR,
XLOG_FATAL,
XLOG_SILENT
} XLogPriority;
.fi

.SS Priority meanings
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
Priority@Purpose
_
XLOG_UNKNOWN@Unknown or unsupported priority
XLOG_DEFAULT@Default logging priority
XLOG_VERBOSE@Detailed diagnostic information
XLOG_DEBUG@Developer/debugging information
XLOG_INFO@General informational messages
XLOG_WARN@Recoverable problems or warnings
XLOG_ERROR@Errors requiring attention
XLOG_FATAL@Fatal conditions or abort scenarios
XLOG_SILENT@Suppresses output
.TE

.B XLOG_UNKNOWN ,
.B XLOG_DEFAULT ,
and
.B XLOG_SILENT
are primarily intended for internal or platform-specific handling.

.SH 5. LOGGING API
The library exposes two core functions:
.BR xlog_vcprint ()
and
.BR xlog_cprint ().

.SS xlog_vcprint()
.nf
XSTDAPI int XCALLAPI xlog_vcprint(
int prio,
int line,
const char *file,
const char *tag,
const char *fmt,
va_list ap
);
.fi

.B xlog_vcprint()
is the
.B va_list
implementation of the logging system.

Arguments are:

.IP \fIprio\fP 12
Log priority.
.IP \fIline\fP 12
Source line number.
.IP \fIfile\fP 12
Source filename.
.IP \fItag\fP 12
Log category or tag.
.IP \fIfmt\fP 12
printf-style format string.
.IP \fIap\fP 12
Variable argument list.

The function returns the result of the underlying formatted output
operation.

.SS xlog_cprint()
.nf
XSTDAPI int XCALLAPI xlog_cprint(
int prio,
int line,
const char *file,
const char *tag,
const char *fmt,
...
);
.fi

.B xlog_cprint()
is the standard variadic logging entry point.

Example:

.nf
xlog_cprint(
XLOG_INFO,
**LINE**,
**FILE**,
"NETWORK",
"Connected to %s",
hostname
);
.fi

In normal application code, the convenience macros should generally be
preferred.

.SH 6. AUTOMATIC SOURCE INFORMATION
The library provides two convenience macros:

.nf
#define xlog_vprint(prio,tag,fmt,ap) \
xlog_vcprint(prio,**LINE**,**FILE**,tag,fmt,ap)

#define xlog_print(prio,tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_cprint(prio,**LINE**,**FILE**,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)
.fi

These macros automatically provide:

.nf
**FILE**
**LINE**
.fi

to the logging implementation.

For example:

.nf
XLOGE("FILE", "Unable to open configuration");
.fi

can produce:

.nf
2026-09-30 18:30:42 [ERROR] FILE (config.c:127) Unable to open configuration
.fi

This eliminates the need for callers to manually provide source-location
information.

.SH 7. STANDARD LOGGING MACROS
The library provides level-specific macros:

.nf
#define XLOGE(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_ERROR,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#define XLOGI(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_INFO,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#define XLOGV(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_VERBOSE,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#define XLOGW(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_WARN,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)

#define XLOGF(tag,fmt,...) \
xlog_print(XLOG_FATAL,tag,fmt,##**VA_ARGS**)
.fi

Example:

.nf
XLOGI("INIT", "Initializing library");
XLOGW("CONFIG", "Configuration file not found");
XLOGE("IO", "Failed to read file: %s", filename);
XLOGF("CORE", "Fatal initialization failure");
.fi

.SH 8. DEBUG LOGGING
Debug logging is conditionally compiled using
.B DEBUG :

.nf
#ifdef DEBUG
# define XLOGD(tag,fmt,...) xlog_print(XLOG_DEBUG,tag,fmt,##__VA_ARGS__)
#else
# define XLOGD(tag,fmt,...) 0
#endif
.fi

When
.B DEBUG
is defined:

.nf
XLOGD("PARSER", "Token count = %d", count);
.fi

generates a debug log.

When
.B DEBUG
is not defined, the macro expands to:

.nf
0
.fi

This allows debug logging to be disabled at compile time.

.SH 9. ANDROID IMPLEMENTATION
Android uses the native Android logging system rather than
.BR stderr .

When:

.nf
**ANDROID**
.fi

is defined, the logger maps
.B XLogPriority
values to Android priorities.

.TS
tab(@);
l l.
XLOG@Android
_
XLOG_DEFAULT@ANDROID_LOG_DEFAULT
XLOG_VERBOSE@ANDROID_LOG_VERBOSE
XLOG_DEBUG@ANDROID_LOG_DEBUG
XLOG_INFO@ANDROID_LOG_INFO
XLOG_WARN@ANDROID_LOG_WARN
XLOG_ERROR@ANDROID_LOG_ERROR
XLOG_FATAL@ANDROID_LOG_FATAL
XLOG_SILENT@ANDROID_LOG_SILENT
Unknown value@ANDROID_LOG_UNKNOWN
.TE

Logging is performed through:

.nf
__android_log_vprint()
.fi

Android therefore receives native Logcat-compatible output.

Source file and line information are not separately emitted by the
non-Android formatting path when Android logging is used.

.SH 10. WINDOWS AND POSIX TIME HANDLING
For non-Android systems,
.B xlog
generates a local timestamp.

On Windows:

.nf
localtime_s(&tm, &now);
.fi

is used.

On POSIX systems:

.nf
localtime_r(&now, &tm);
.fi

is used.

These functions avoid the non-thread-safe
.BR localtime (3)
interface.

The timestamp is formatted using:

.nf
strftime(
timestamp,
sizeof(timestamp),
"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S",
&tm
);
.fi

Example:

.nf
2026-09-30 18:30:42
.fi

.SH 11. DESKTOP/POSIX OUTPUT FORMAT
Non-Android output follows the general structure:

.nf
TIMESTAMP [PRIORITY] TAG (FILE:LINE) MESSAGE
.fi

For example:

.nf
2026-09-30 18:30:42 [INFO] XLOG (main.c:42) Library initialized
.fi

If no source location is available:

.nf
2026-09-30 18:30:42 [INFO] XLOG Library initialized
.fi

The logger terminates every message with a newline and flushes the
configured output stream:

.nf
fputc('\en', XLOG_STREAM);
fflush(XLOG_STREAM);
.fi

This ensures that log messages are immediately delivered to the
configured stream.

.SH 12. SILENT LOGGING
.B XLOG_SILENT
disables output:

.nf
if (prio == XLOG_SILENT) {
...
return 0;
}
.fi

It can therefore be used to explicitly suppress a logging operation.

Android maps this priority to:

.nf
ANDROID_LOG_SILENT
.fi

.SH 13. VARIADIC ARGUMENT HANDLING
The logger uses
.B va_list
internally so that both public logging functions can share the same
implementation.

The implementation creates a copy:

.nf
va_list apc;
va_copy(apc, ap);
.fi

The copied argument list is then passed to the underlying formatted
output function.

This allows the original
.B va_list
ownership to remain with the caller while
.B xlog_vcprint()
consumes its own copy.

The copied list is released with:

.nf
va_end(apc);
.fi

.SH 14. EXAMPLE USAGE
A typical application can include the library through:

.nf
#include "libxc.h"
.fi

and then use:

.nf
int main(void) {
    XLOGI("MAIN", "Application started");
    int value = 42;
    XLOGD("MAIN", "value = %d", value);
    XLOGW("MAIN", "This is a warning");
    XLOGE("MAIN", "This is an error");

    return 0;
}
.fi

Example output:

.nf
2026-09-30 18:30:42 [INFO] MAIN (main.c:3) Application started
2026-09-30 18:30:42 [DEBUG] MAIN (main.c:7) value = 42
2026-09-30 18:30:42 [Warn] MAIN (main.c:8) This is a warning
2026-09-30 18:30:42 [ERROR] MAIN (main.c:9) This is an error
.fi

.B XLOGD
appears only when the library is compiled with
.B DEBUG
defined.

.SH 15. PLATFORM BEHAVIOR
.TS
tab(@);
l l l l.
Platform@Output mechanism@Timestamp@Source location
_
Windows@XLOG_STREAM / stderr@Yes@Yes
POSIX@XLOG_STREAM / stderr@Yes@Yes
Android@Android Logcat@Android-managed@Not separately printed
C++@Same C API through extern "C"@Platform-dependent@Platform-dependent
.TE

The public logging interface remains the same regardless of platform.

.SH 16. C++ COMPATIBILITY
The exported functions are wrapped in:

.nf
#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif
.fi

and:

.nf
#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif
.fi

This prevents C++ name mangling and allows the library to be called
directly from C++ applications.

For example:

.nf
#include "xlog.h"

int main() {
    XLOGI("CPP", "Hello from C++");
}
.fi

The underlying functions retain C linkage.

.SH 17. ABI AND EXPORT MACROS
The logging functions use the Extern Library API abstraction:

.nf
XSTDAPI
XCALLAPI
.fi

For example:

.nf
XSTDAPI int XCALLAPI xlog_cprint(...);
.fi

These macros allow the library to control:

.IP (bu 2
Symbol visibility.
.IP (bu 2
DLL exports and imports.
.IP (bu 2
Calling conventions.
.IP (bu 2
Platform-specific ABI requirements.

The exact behavior of these macros is provided by the library's
portability headers.

.SH 18. DESIGN GOALS
.B xlog.h
is designed around several principles:

.IP (bu 2
.B Small API
(em only two core logging functions.
.IP (bu 2
.B Cross-platform behavior
(em Windows, POSIX, and Android support.
.IP (bu 2
.B Source diagnostics
(em automatic file and line information.
.IP (bu 2
.B Log levels
(em standardized priority classification.
.IP (bu 2
.B Tags
(em logical grouping of messages.
.IP (bu 2
.B Debug elimination
(em debug messages can be compiled out.
.IP (bu 2
.B C ABI
(em usable from both C and C++.
.IP (bu 2
.B Existing library integration
(em uses
.BR XSTDAPI ,
.BR XCALLAPI ,
and
.BR xbasename (3).
.IP (bu 2
.B Immediate output
(em streams are flushed after each message.

.SH 19. HEADER INTEGRATION
.B xlog.h
is included by the umbrella library header:

.nf
#include "xlog.h"
.fi

For example:

.nf
#ifndef LIBXC_H
#define LIBXC_H

#include "xlocale.h"
#include "xlog.h"
#include "xstdio.h"
#include "xstdlib.h"
#include "xstring.h"
#include "xwchar.h"
#include "xmap.h"
#include "xctype.h"
#include "xwctype.h"
#include "xstat.h"
#include "xdlfcn.h"

#endif
.fi

This makes the logging API available to applications through the main
.B libxc.h
interface.

