.SH 21. SUMMARY
.B xlog.h
provides the Extern Library with a unified logging layer across desktop
and mobile platforms.

Its primary interface consists of:

.nf
xlog_vcprint()
xlog_cprint()
.fi

and the convenience macros:

.nf
XLOGV()
XLOGD()
XLOGI()
XLOGW()
XLOGE()
XLOGF()
.fi

Applications can therefore use one logging API while the library handles
platform-specific output, timestamp generation, priority mapping,
source information, and debug configuration.

.SH RETURN VALUES
.B xlog_vcprint()
and
.B xlog_cprint()
return the result of the underlying formatted output operation.

When
.B XLOGD
is compiled without
.B DEBUG ,
it expands to
.BR 0 .

.B XLOG_SILENT
logging returns zero without producing output.

.SH PORTABILITY
The XLOG interface is designed for:

.IP (bu 2
Windows.
.IP (bu 2
POSIX-compatible systems.
.IP (bu 2
Android.
.IP (bu 2
C++ applications using the C ABI.

Windows uses
.BR localtime_s (3)
for local time conversion, while POSIX systems use
.BR localtime_r (3).

Android uses the native Android logging API and does not use the desktop
timestamp and stream formatting path.

.SH THREAD SAFETY
The logger avoids the non-thread-safe
.BR localtime (3)
function by using
.B localtime_s()
on Windows and
.B localtime_r()
on POSIX systems.

The logging functions themselves write to the configured output stream
on desktop and POSIX platforms. Applications requiring serialized output
from multiple threads should provide appropriate synchronization around
shared logging destinations when necessary.

.SH ERRORS
The logging interface does not define a separate error-reporting
mechanism.

Formatting and output errors are reported through the return value of
the underlying formatted output operation.

No additional error information is provided by the XLOG API.

